<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Kremnica</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Körmöcbánya, Kremnitz</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Kremnica is a mining town in central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slovakia">Slovakia</a>, called Körmöcbánya in the medieval <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-hungary">Kingdom of Hungary</a>. Its gold mines made it one of the richest towns in the kingdom, and the mint founded there in 1328 struck the Hungarian gold florin and later ducats. The mint still operates, making it one of the oldest continuously working mints in the world.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/kremnica.jpg" alt="Kremnica" width="320" height="236" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Dušan Turan · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Kremnica_v_zime.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Kremnica lies in the Kremnica Mountains, where abundant gold ore made it one of the major mining towns of the world in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">Middle Ages</a> and the modern era. The first evidence of underground mining there dates from the 9th century. After the Mongol invasion of the 13th century, Hungarian kings invited colonists from present-day <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>, including Zipser Germans, whose technology restored the mines. The first written reference to the town dates from 1328, when King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-i-of-hungary">Charles I of Hungary</a> granted it royal town privileges; a mint already existed then.</p><p>From 1335 the mint produced gold florins and later the Kremnica ducats, used as an international means of payment because their gold was consistently pure. In 1331 the town became the seat of the Chamber Count, who administered all the mines and mints in twelve counties of the kingdom.</p><p>At the end of the 14th century Kremnica became the capital of the mining towns of central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/upper-hungary">Upper Hungary</a>. In the 15th century it was the second most important city in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-hungary">Kingdom of Hungary</a>, and its deputies held second place in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hungarian-diet">Hungarian Diet</a>, after those of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buda">Buda</a>. Gold mining and coin production peaked in the 14th and 15th centuries. The town was one of the main centres of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protestant-reformation">Reformation</a> in the country and belonged to the Protestant League of Seven Mining Towns.</p><p>In the 16th century it was a centre for medals, mostly religious ones, especially by Joachim Deschler. By then miners had to dig deeper and water in the shafts made mining more costly and less profitable. The last gold was extracted in 1970, when all the mines closed. The German population of the town and its surroundings was expelled in 1945 by the Czechoslovak government after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Kremnica is a town in central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slovakia">Slovakia</a> on the brook Kremnický potok, a tributary of the Hron, with 4,586 inhabitants. A castle built between the 13th and 15th centuries towers over the town and is a well-preserved fortification system, with a double wall. At the end of the 15th century the Chamber Count moved to a new house, and his residence in the castle became St Catherine's Church. The town also has a Museum of Coins and Medals, a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baroque">Baroque</a> plague column on the main square and a 15th-century aqueduct about 20 kilometres long that still supplies hydroelectric power plants. The mint is the oldest still-working mint in the world.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Kremnica” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Gold from <b>Kremnica</b> and Transylvania and silver from Banská Štiavnica made Hungary the leading producer of precious metals in Europe for much of the fourteenth century, and Charles I's gold florin, minted from 1325, became one of the continent's standard coins.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-hungary-1301-1526">Kingdom of Hungary (1301-1526) · Socioeconomy</a></li></ul></section>
